An array is basically a way to store a bunch of items, usually packed together in one contiguous chunk of memory. The items are normally all the same type, and you grab any one of them by its index. In most languages an array has a fixed size, and each slot gets its own index, starting at 0 for the first one, 1 for the second, and so on.

Here's how you'd declare and fill an array of integers in C:

int numbers[5];
numbers[0] = 10;
numbers[1] = 20;
numbers[2] = 30;
numbers[3] = 40;
numbers[4] = 50;

So that makes an array called numbers that holds 5 ints, and we're setting each slot one by one. To read them back, just use the index:

printf("%d", numbers[0]); // prints 10
printf("%d", numbers[2]); // prints 30

Arrays are great for handling lots of data, but they've got some downsides. Since the size is locked in when you create it, you can't just tack on or drop elements whenever you feel like it. And if you need to insert or delete something in the middle, arrays are kind of clunky at that. If you run into that stuff a lot, a linked list might be a better fit.
